---
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import { homeSchema } from '../data/demos';
import Opener from '../components/sections/Opener.astro';
import ProofBar from '../components/sections/ProofBar.astro';
import CheckBand from '../components/sections/CheckBand.astro';
import LeadOffer from '../components/sections/LeadOffer.astro';
import Offers from '../components/sections/Offers.astro';
import AssessmentBand from '../components/sections/AssessmentBand.astro';
import Breadth from '../components/sections/Breadth.astro';
import ToolsDoorway from '../components/sections/ToolsDoorway.astro';
import Cta from '../components/sections/Cta.astro';
import { shapesHead } from '../data/home';

const origin = new URL(Astro.site!).origin;
---
<MarketingLayout
  title="AppliedIQ Solutions"
  description="Custom software for small local business — a real website you own, your spreadsheets turned into a live tool, and a shelf of finished tools that run on your own rules in your own browser. Built by an operator who does this at industrial scale. Yours to own, not rented."
  schema={homeSchema(origin)}
>
  {/* THE PITCH, not a navigation hub (D29). A visitor arriving from Google, a
      business card, or Nextdoor lands on the offer — not on a menu asking them
      to pick a door. That reversal is the whole point of this page.

      Order is load-bearing. The offer (LeadOffer, Offers) sits ABOVE the
      industrial proof (Breadth's at-scale links), because a proof strip leading
      with manufacturing scale, read before any offer, tells a shop owner this
      is an enterprise site. If this order ever slips back to proof-first, the
      pivot fails quietly.

      Confirmation Outlook is NOT a section here and no longer has a portal tile
      in the Opener — it is a deployed enterprise service, which is exactly the
      thing this page stopped selling. It keeps its own page, reached from the
      Work nav group and from /work/'s proof band. It is also not in demos.ts:
      proofStats says "open any one in your browser", true of the islands and
      false of it.

      The old LiveTools section is GONE. It made the same proof-at-scale
      argument the shape grid below already makes, for 763px on a page that
      measured 9,462px on a phone — and this page's whole history is it growing
      back (12,696px in July). Its caliber claim moved into the Shapes lede and
      its one door to /work/ hangs in Breadth's foot slot.

      MEASURED 9,738px ON 2026-09-03, after the assessment band. The band cost
      276px, which is the price of the paid offer being reachable from this page
      at all. Re-measure with `node scripts/measure-pages.mjs` before the next
      section rather than trusting either figure here — that is the whole reason
      no threshold is written down.

      Indices read /01–/02. Copy lives in src/data/home.ts. */}
  <Opener />
  {/* THE CHECK TAKES THE SLOT STRAIGHT AFTER THE OPENER, as the plan reserved.
      It is the lead offer, and it is the only thing on this page that works on a
      visitor who has never heard of custom software: two numbers in, one number
      about THEIR business back, with the arithmetic printed.

      It sits ABOVE the proof bar deliberately. A visitor who has just been told
      what a stranger's career was is more likely to act on something they can do
      than on more reasons to believe him. */}
  <CheckBand />
  <ProofBar />
  {/* THE OFFER COMES BEFORE THE PROOF OF IT, reordered 2026-08-24.
      Review Autopilot used to sit here as "the lead offer", which stopped
      being true when a platform shipped its whole category built in and free.
      It is now what it actually is: a working tool a visitor can open, sitting
      UNDER the three things actually being sold rather than in front of them.

      When the free instant check ships it takes the slot directly after the
      opener, and this comment is the note that says so. */}
  <Offers />
  {/* THE ASSESSMENT SITS HERE AND NOWHERE HIGHER, added 2026-09-03. It answers
      the question Offers above leaves behind — a reader has just seen the three
      things that get built and may not know which one is theirs. That is what
      the assessment sells, so it belongs immediately after and nowhere else.

      It stays BELOW the free check on purpose. The check is the lead offer and
      the only thing on this page a stranger can act on; a paid item above it
      would pull the reader out of the funnel at the point it is meant to draw
      them in. The band names no figure, which is what keeps it one band tall. */}
  <AssessmentBand />
  <LeadOffer />
  <Breadth
    id="shapes"
    tone="canvas"
    index={shapesHead.index}
    eyebrow={shapesHead.eyebrow}
    title={shapesHead.title}
    lede={shapesHead.lede}
    columns={2}
    showAreas={false}
    showScales={true}
  >
    <ToolsDoorway />
  </Breadth>
  <Cta />
</MarketingLayout>
